# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3 have "laws_addressed_variously" and "laws_addressed_variously → laws_bind_addressees"; hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.2 has "laws_addressed_variously"; they differ when only "some Laws are addressed to all the Subjects in generall, some to particular Provinces, some to particular Vocations, and some to particular Men" is true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "some Laws are addressed to all the Subjects in generall, some to particular Provinces, some to particular Vocations, and some to particular Men" is true and "Lawes are Lawes to every of those to whom the Command is directed, and to none else" is false. Is S2, "For every man seeth, that some Lawes are addressed to all the Subjects in generall; some to particular Provinces; some to particular Vocations; and some to particular Men; and are therefore Lawes, to every of those to whom the Command is directed; and to none else.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3 have "nothing_unjust_without_law" and "nothing_unjust_without_law → laws_rules_just"; hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.2 has "nothing_unjust_without_law"; they differ when only "nothing is reputed Unjust that is not contrary to some Law" is true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "nothing is reputed Unjust that is not contrary to some Law" is true and "Lawes are the Rules of Just and Unjust" is false. Is S3, "As also, that Lawes are the Rules of Just, and Unjust; nothing being reputed Unjust, that is not contrary to some Law.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3 have "subjection_to_cw_only" and "subjection_to_cw_only → only_cw_makes_laws" and "know_obey_only_by_signs" and "know_obey_only_by_signs → commands_signified"; hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.2 has "subjection_to_cw_only" and "know_obey_only_by_signs"; they differ when only "our Subjection is to the Common-wealth only", "none can make Lawes but the Common-wealth" and "a man knows not otherwise than by sufficient Signs how to obey Commands" are true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "our Subjection is to the Common-wealth only", "none can make Lawes but the Common-wealth" and "a man knows not otherwise than by sufficient Signs how to obey Commands" are true and "Commands are to be signified by sufficient Signs" is false. Is S4, "Likewise, that none can make Lawes but the Common-wealth; because our Subjection is to the Common-wealth only: and that Commands, are to be signified by sufficient Signs; because a man knows not otherwise how to obey them.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.3 have "laws_bind_addressees ∧ laws_rules_just ∧ only_cw_makes_laws ∧ commands_signified → def_evident" and "def_evident → deductions_true"; hobbes-leviathan-1.logic.r1.2 has "laws_addressed_variously → laws_bind_addressees" and "nothing_unjust_without_law → laws_rules_just" and "subjection_to_cw_only → only_cw_makes_laws" and "know_obey_only_by_signs → commands_signified" and "laws_bind_addressees ∧ laws_rules_just ∧ only_cw_makes_laws ∧ commands_signified → def_evident" and "def_evident → deductions_true"; they differ when only "some Laws are addressed to all the Subjects in generall, some to particular Provinces, some to particular Vocations, and some to particular Men", "Lawes are Lawes to every of those to whom the Command is directed, and to none else", "nothing is reputed Unjust that is not contrary to some Law", "Lawes are the Rules of Just and Unjust", "our Subjection is to the Common-wealth only", "none can make Lawes but the Common-wealth", "a man knows not otherwise than by sufficient Signs how to obey Commands", "in the definition of law there is nothing that is not at first sight evident" and "whatsoever can from this definition by necessary consequence be deduced ought to be acknowledged for truth" are true, which the first allows
